ing_date":"2026-03-04","published":"2026-02-24","filename":"E06000006-halton-2026-03-04-council-81876-exec-board-2026-27-budget-report-v11.pdf"},{"id":82176,"title":"PensionsDiscretions report 2627 FINAL","url":"http://councillors.halton.gov.uk/documents/s82176/pensionsdiscretions-report-2627-final.pdf","type":"other","type_label":"Paper","weight":1,"committee":"Executive Board","meeting_id":9183,"meeting_date":"2026-04-16","published":"2026-04-07","filename":"E06000006-halton-2026-04-16-executive-board-82176-pensionsdiscretions-report-2627-final.pdf"}],"notes":{"gss":"E06000006","schema":2,"council":"Halton","facts":[{"key":"cfr","value":"NOT STATED \u2014 only the movement (\u00a34.992m increase) is given","as_at":"2026-03-31","source":{"title":"Treasury Management Annual Report 2025-26 \u2014 Executive Board, 9 July 2026","url":"https://moderngov.halton.gov.uk/documents/s82804/Treasury%20Management%20Annual%20Report%202025-26.pdf","doc_id":82804,"drive_id":"1AwH4N6YLCk1wgJpNMtPsgwmLtxAjiXXp","text":"papers/E06000006/82804.txt"},"note":"Appendix 1. No CFR level appears anywhere in the report, so internal borrowing CANNOT be computed and MUST NOT be asserted."},{"key":"external_borrowing","value":"\u00a3252m total borrowing, flat at every month end September to March; external debt including PFI and leases \u00a3624.982m","as_at":"2026-03-31","source":{"title":"Treasury Management Annual Report 2025-26 \u2014 Executive Board, 9 July 2026","url":"https://moderngov.halton.gov.uk/documents/s82804/Treasury%20Management%20Annual%20Report%202025-26.pdf","doc_id":82804,"drive_id":"1AwH4N6YLCk1wgJpNMtPsgwmLtxAjiXXp","text":"papers/E06000006/82804.txt"},"note":"Month-end table and Appendix 1. Against an updated Operational Boundary of \u00a3644.536m and an Authorised Limit of \u00a3714.944m. Complied."},{"key":"loan_book","value":"10 PWLB loans, \u00a3212,000,000, weighted 3.891%","as_at":"2026-03-31","source":{"title":"Treasury Management Annual Report 2025-26 \u2014 Executive Board, 9 July 2026","url":"https://moderngov.halton.gov.uk/documents/s82804/Treasury%20Management%20Annual%20Report%202025-26.pdf","doc_id":82804,"drive_id":"1AwH4N6YLCk1wgJpNMtPsgwmLtxAjiXXp","text":"papers/E06000006/82804.txt"},"note":"OUR figure from bc-loans. Five \u00a310m loans at 4.78%\u20134.90% maturing 10 April to 15 May 2026, and five long loans totalling \u00a3162m: \u00a355m at 3.78% and \u00a358m at 3.97% both to 31 March 2044, \u00a329m at 2.73% to 2047, \u00a310m at 3.70% to 2056 and \u00a310m at 2.87% to 2066."},{"key":"refinancing","value":"\u00a350m of PWLB drawn February\u2013March 2025 matures between 10 April and 15 May 2026 and has had to be replaced in early 2026/27","as_at":"2026-03-31","source":{"title":"Treasury Management Annual Report 2025-26 \u2014 Executive Board, 9 July 2026","url":"https://moderngov.halton.gov.uk/documents/s82804/Treasury%20Management%20Annual%20Report%202025-26.pdf","doc_id":82804,"drive_id":"1AwH4N6YLCk1wgJpNMtPsgwmLtxAjiXXp","text":"papers/E06000006/82804.txt"},"note":"Their \u00a73.12, verbatim: replaced \"after rates had risen due to the conflict in the Middle East, and will be reflected in higher borrowing costs in 2026/27\". OUR FILE TIES IT: five \u00a310m loans, weighted 4.836%, maturing exactly in that window. Principal, count and timing all match."},{"key":"break_cost","value":"the five long loans (\u00a3162,000,000) are ALL in discount \u2014 about \u00a336.137m; indicative settlement \u00a3125.863m","as_at":"2026-03-31","source":{"title":"Treasury Management Annual Report 2025-26 \u2014 Executive Board, 9 July 2026","url":"https://moderngov.halton.gov.uk/documents/s82804/Treasury%20Management%20Annual%20Report%202025-26.pdf","doc_id":82804,"drive_id":"1AwH4N6YLCk1wgJpNMtPsgwmLtxAjiXXp","text":"papers/E06000006/82804.txt"},"note":"DMO curve, notice 314/26 PM, 14 August 2026. The five short loans had already matured by our calculation date and cannot be priced. INDICATIVE ONLY \u2014 only the DMO can quote."},{"key":"treasury_outturn","value":"\u00a31.810m under budget \u2014 investment income over target by \u00a31.691m and borrowing costs under by \u00a30.119m","as_at":"2026-03-31","source":{"title":"Treasury Management Annual Report 2025-26 \u2014 Executive Board, 9 July 2026","url":"https://moderngov.halton.gov.uk/documents/s82804/Treasury%20Management%20Annual%20Report%202025-26.pdf","doc_id":82804,"drive_id":"1AwH4N6YLCk1wgJpNMtPsgwmLtxAjiXXp","text":"papers/E06000006/82804.txt"},"note":"\u00a73.11 and the net interest table. Budget \u00a31.752m against an actual net credit of \u00a30.058m. The table foots exactly."},{"key":"investment_performance","value":"beat the benchmark at every duration of one month and over; total interest earned \u00a32.193m October to March","as_at":"2026-03-31","source":{"title":"Treasury Management Annual Report 2025-26 \u2014 Executive Board, 9 July 2026","url":"https://moderngov.halton.gov.uk/documents/s82804/Treasury%20Management%20Annual%20Report%202025-26.pdf","doc_id":82804,"drive_id":"1AwH4N6YLCk1wgJpNMtPsgwmLtxAjiXXp","text":"papers/E06000006/82804.txt"},"note":"\u00a73.10. The one-day return of 3.71% was below the 3.79% benchmark because call account rates lag. Nine-month money returned 4.67% against a 4.01% benchmark."},{"key":"ifrs16","value":"IFRS 16 restatement of two PFI liabilities \u2014 The Grange School +\u00a34.7m and the Mersey Gateway Bridge +\u00a346.1m \u2014 breached the Operational Boundary at the end of 2024/25","as_at":"2026-03-31","source":{"title":"Treasury Management Annual Report 2025-26 \u2014 Executive Board, 9 July 2026","url":"https://moderngov.halton.gov.uk/documents/s82804/Treasury%20Management%20Annual%20Report%202025-26.pdf","doc_id":82804,"drive_id":"1AwH4N6YLCk1wgJpNMtPsgwmLtxAjiXXp","text":"papers/E06000006/82804.txt"},"note":"\u00a73.17. Both the Operational Boundary and the Authorised Limit were updated in March 2026. Portsmouth reports the same pattern on its Highways PFI: a presentational change with a real indicator consequence."},{"key":"rates_match","value":"their March 2026 PWLB rates \u2014 1yr 5.04%, 10yr 5.72%, 25yr 6.29% \u2014 match our own 31 March 2026 certainty series to the basis point on all three","as_at":"2026-03-31","source":{"title":"Treasury Management Annual Report 2025-26 \u2014 Executive Board, 9 July 2026","url":"https://moderngov.halton.gov.uk/documents/s82804/Treasury%20Management%20Annual%20Report%202025-26.pdf","doc_id":82804,"drive_id":"1AwH4N6YLCk1wgJpNMtPsgwmLtxAjiXXp","text":"papers/E06000006/82804.txt"},"note":"\u00a73.8 rate table. A MUFG client, so the agreement is across advisers rather than within one."},{"key":"financing_cost_ratio","value":"5.7% of net revenue stream against a 7.8% estimate","as_at":"2026-03-31","source":{"title":"Treasury Management Annual Report 2025-26 \u2014 Executive Board, 9 July 2026","url":"https://moderngov.halton.gov.uk/documents/s82804/Treasury%20Management%20Annual%20Report%202025-26.pdf","doc_id":82804,"drive_id":"1AwH4N6YLCk1wgJpNMtPsgwmLtxAjiXXp","text":"papers/E06000006/82804.txt"},"note":"Appendix 1. Up from 3.5% in 2024/25. Capital expenditure \u00a358.652m against a \u00a369.614m estimate."}],"threads":[],"meetings":{},"summary":{"written":"14 August 2026","text":"<p>The live item is paragraph 3.12. Halton borrowed \u00a350m from the PWLB in February and March 2025 when rates fell, and the report says that money \"has had to be replaced in early 2026/27 after rates had risen\", with higher borrowing costs to follow. Our file shows exactly that money \u2014 five \u00a310m loans at 4.78% to 4.90%, weighted 4.836%, maturing between 10 April and 15 May 2026.</p><p>The useful part is that the number has moved since they wrote. Their own March column shows the one-year PWLB rate at 5.04%. On 14 August it is 4.95%, so the replacement is running about 11 basis points above the maturing coupon rather than the larger step the March framing implied \u2014 roughly \u00a357,000 a year on \u00a350m.</p><p>No CFR level is stated anywhere, only the \u00a34.992m movement, so internal borrowing cannot be computed and nothing is asserted about it. The net interest table foots exactly and their three March PWLB rates match our series to the basis point.</p><p>Second thread: an IFRS 16 restatement of the Grange School and Mersey Gateway PFI liabilities (+\u00a34.7m and +\u00a346.1m) breached the Operational Boundary at the end of 2024/25. Portsmouth reports the same pattern. That is now a sector story rather than a local one.</p>"},"courses":{"scored":false,"read":["treasury_annual"],"text_held":true},"contacts":[{"name":null,"role":"Director of Finance","email":null,"note":"The only attribution is \"REPORTING OFFICER: Director of Finance\". Not named, no address. Do not guess."}],"status":{"state":"needs-address","written_up":true,"contact":false,"as_at":"14 August 2026","source_text_held":true,"note":"Read from Drive; paper text at papers/E06000006/82804.txt. NO CFR LEVEL is published, so internal borrowing is not asserted. DRAFT WRITTEN AND MACHINE-CHECKED \u2014 recipient line is the only gap; the Director of Finance is not named in the report.","state_was":"drafted"},"emails":{"officer":{"subject":"The \u00a350m you're replacing \u2014 the rate moved in your favour since March","to":"","cc":"","written":"14 August 2026","send_by":"2026-09-18","meeting":"Executive Board / Audit and Governance Board \u2014 next treasury report","state":"ready-no-address","market":{"as_at":"2026-08-14","pwlb_notice":"314/26 (PM fixing)","basis":"MATURITY profile, CERTAINTY rates (published standard less 0.20)","pwlb_1y":4.95,"pwlb_2y":5.06,"pwlb_5y":5.34,"pwlb_10y":5.83,"pwlb_20y":6.38,"pwlb_25y":6.46,"pwlb_50y":6.27,"bank_rate":3.75,"ois_1y":4.11,"ois_2y":4.21,"july_activity":"\u00a3368m across 33 loans from 19 borrowers, average 5.02%","repayment_curve":"/api/data/bc-repayment-full, REPAYMENT, notice 314/26 PM, 14 Aug 2026","loan_file":"/api/data/bc-loans, sheet edited 2026-08-03"},"voice":"EMAIL_TEMPLATE.md","contacts_source":"See contacts[]","placeholders":["[BROCHURE URL]","[OFFICER NAME]","[OFFICER EMAIL]"],"links":["pwlbtoday.org/loan-analysis","pwlbtoday.org/pwlb-activity","pwlbtoday.org/academy"],"courses_named":["Leases on the balance sheet (IFRS 16)","Strategic PWLB borrowing","Reading the PWLBpredict","PWLB break costs \u2014 mechanics & live calculator"],"reconciliation":"Five \u00a310m PWLB loans at 4.78%\u20134.90%, weighted 4.836%, maturing 10 April to 15 May 2026 \u2014 exactly the \u00a350m of \u00a73.12. Ten loans in total, \u00a3212,000,000 at 3.891%. Net interest table foots exactly (1,691 + 119 = 1,810). Their March PWLB rates match our series on all three.","sources":["papers/E06000006/82804.txt","/api/data/bc-loans","outreach/canon_break_cost.py at notice 314/26 PM"],"body":"Hi [OFFICER NAME],\n\nApologies for the cold email. PWLBtoday is fairly new so you may not have come across us yet.\n\nWe're built for local authority treasury teams and nothing else: live PWLB rates at both daily fixings, a monthly breakdown of every loan the sector draws, every council's PWLB book priced daily against the DMO's repayment curve, and a training academy for officers and members. There's more \u2014 the predictor, the SONIA path, peer metrics, a daily brief \u2014 in the brochure at [BROCHURE URL]. We don't sell treasury products, and the data side is free and stays free.\n\nTo keep that data and our training relevant we built tools that watch committee papers across every council in the country, so we can see how a council's own figures compare with where the market has gone since. So below are a few thoughts on the annual report that went to Executive Board on 9 July. You're not being singled out \u2014 every authority gets one of these \u2014 and there's no agenda in it.\n\nA cross-check first. We hold ten PWLB loans for Halton, \u00a3212,000,000 at a weighted 3.891%. Five of them are \u00a310m each at 4.78% to 4.90%, maturing between 10 April and 15 May 2026 \u2014 which is exactly the \u00a350m your paragraph 3.12 describes, drawn in February and March 2025 and needing replacement in early 2026/27. Principal, count and timing all line up, so we are confident we are looking at the same money.\n\nThat paragraph is why we wrote, and the reason is that the number has moved since you wrote it. Your own March column puts the one-year PWLB rate at 5.04%. On this afternoon's fixing, notice 314/26, one-year certainty is 4.95%. The five maturing loans are weighted at 4.836%. So the replacement is running roughly 11 basis points above the money it replaces \u2014 about \u00a357,000 a year on \u00a350m \u2014 rather than the larger step the March position implied. Two-year is 5.06% and five-year 5.34% if any of it goes longer.\n\nWe are not suggesting what to do with that and we are in no position to; you will know the cashflow shape far better than we can from a report. The point is only that \"rates had risen\" was true in March and is a little less true in August, and the difference is worth having in front of you when the replacement is actually transacted rather than when the report was drafted. Rates fix twice daily and we publish both, with the forward curve alongside. July's sector lending was \u00a3368m across 33 loans from just 19 borrowers at an average 5.02%, which is thin \u2014 worth knowing when you are the one bringing \u00a350m to the market. That breakdown is at pwlbtoday.org/pwlb-activity.\n\nA small note on scope. Your Appendix 1 gives the movement in the CFR but not the level, so we have not calculated internal borrowing and nothing above depends on it.\n\nTwo other things. Your March PWLB rates \u2014 5.04% at one year, 5.72% at ten and 6.29% at twenty-five \u2014 match our own 31 March series to the basis point on all three. You are a MUFG client and several of the councils where we have seen the same agreement are Arlingclose, so that is agreement across advisers rather than inside one.\n\nAnd the IFRS 16 restatement at 3.17 is worth flagging as sector context. The Grange School at +\u00a34.7m and Mersey Gateway at +\u00a346.1m moving the PFI liability enough to breach the Operational Boundary at the end of 2024/25 is not unusual \u2014 Portsmouth reports the same thing on its Highways PFI, where an indexation adjustment pushed the figure above the limits in the same presentational way. It catches people out precisely because nothing economic has happened. Ours is a cross-council view, so we tend to see these patterns before they get written about.\n\nOn the long half of the book, all five loans beyond 2044 price below par on the DMO's premature repayment curve today \u2014 about \u00a336.1m of discount on \u00a3162m. Nothing follows from that: we don't sell treasury products, we model no MRP or accounting treatment, and only the DMO can quote. Yours is at pwlbtoday.org/loan-analysis.\n\nOn training, four fit Halton. Leases on the balance sheet (IFRS 16) goes straight to 3.17. Strategic PWLB borrowing and Reading the PWLBpredict for the \u00a350m replacement decision. And PWLB break costs \u2014 mechanics & live calculator. All 115 courses are free to local authorities until the end of September at pwlbtoday.org/academy, and free accounts for the whole team.\n\nAnd if you do find it useful, mentioning it to others would help us more than anything. We're small, word of mouth is most of our marketing, and every sign-up helps us prove the need.\n\nSign up free \u2192 https://pwlbtoday.org/\nFollow on LinkedIn \u2192 https://www.linkedin.com/company/pwlbtoday/\n\nRussell\nhttps://www.linkedin.com/in/russelltew\n\nSource: Treasury Management Annual Report 2025-26, Executive Board, 9 July 2026."}}}}